 <h3 style="margin-top:22px">Rules for taking the photos</h3>
 <ul class="rules">
  <li>The first flash waits for the turntable's "in position" signal, not for a fixed time.</li>
  <li>One camera and one light at a time, in the order set in the recipe. With the B side up, the recipe switches CAM 2, CAM 3 and CAM 4 off.</li>
  <li>Each light is switched by its own camera, so the flash always lines up with that camera's shutter.</li>
  <li>Photos are sent to the vision PC while the table turns to the next stop, so sending does not slow the machine down.</li>
  <li>If a photo does not arrive, the camera takes it again once. If it is still missing, the part goes to REVIEW. A part is never passed with a photo missing.</li>
  <li>The machine is closed with covers, so room light cannot change the photos.</li>
 </ul>
</section>

<section id="pipeline">
 <h2>From part in to result</h2>
 <p>The full order of work for one part.</p>
 <div class="pipe">
  <div class="g1"><h3>On the machine</h3><ol>
   <li><span class="n">1</span><div><b>Part comes in</b>A sensor sees the part. The machine gives it a part number and notes the time.</div></li>
   <li><span class="n">2</span><div><b>Recipe loads</b>The recipe for this impeller model sets the stops, the camera order, and the shutter time, flash length and brightness for each camera.</div></li>
   <li><span class="n">3</span><div><b>Station 1, A side</b>1 photo from the top camera, lit by its panel light.</div></li>
   <li><span class="n">4</span><div><b>Station 2, A side</b>8 stops with 4 photos each: 32 photos.</div></li>
   <li><span class="n">5</span><div><b>Turn over, B side</b>8 stops at Station 2 with only CAM 5: 8 photos of the bore. Then 1 photo at Station 1. In total: 42 photos per part.</div></li>
  </ol></div>
  <div class="g2"><h3>On the vision PC</h3><ol>
   <li><span class="n">6</span><div><b>Check each photo</b>Is it there, sharp and bright enough? If not, take it again once.</div></li>
   <li><span class="n">7</span><div><b>Save the original</b>Before anything else, named by part number, round, stop and camera.</div></li>
   <li><span class="n">8</span><div><b>AI finds defects</b>On full-size tiles of each photo, so small pin holes are not lost.</div></li>
   <li><span class="n">9</span><div><b>Merge repeats</b>A defect seen by both 0° cameras, or at two stops next to each other, is counted once. With two gap cameras this matters more than before.</div></li>
  </ol></div>
  <div class="g3"><h3>Decision and record</h3><ol>
   <li><span class="n">10</span><div><b>Quality rules decide</b>Rules for each zone, from the Aquasub quality manual. Result: PASS, FAIL or REVIEW.</div></li>
   <li><span class="n">11</span><div><b>Sort the part</b>The part goes to its lane.</div></li>
   <li><span class="n">12</span><div><b>Keep the record</b>The result, the marked photos and the recipe version are saved with the part number.</div></li>
  </ol></div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="result">
 <h2>How the machine decides</h2>
 <div class="res">
  <div class="p"><span class="word">PASS</span><p>No problem found. The part is good.</p></div>
  <div class="f"><span class="word">FAIL</span><p>A defect is too big, there are too many, or it is in a place where no defect is allowed.</p></div>
  <div class="r"><span class="word">REVIEW</span><p>The machine is not sure, or a photo is missing. A person looks at the part and decides.</p></div>
 </div>
 <div class="who"><p><b>Who decides?</b> The AI only finds and marks the defects. The final answer comes from fixed quality rules taken from the Aquasub quality manual.</p></div>
 <h3 style="margin-top:28px">The main rules, in simple words</h3>
 <ul class="simple">
  <li>On most surfaces, one small hole up to 1 mm is allowed. Two holes means FAIL.</li>
  <li>On the outside of the neck ring, no hole and no crack is allowed.</li>
  <li>A crack anywhere means FAIL.</li>
  <li>A welding mark on a machined surface means FAIL.</li>
  <li>A missing, dark or blurred photo is never a PASS. The part goes to REVIEW.</li>
 </ul>
 <div class="example">
  <img src="data:image/webp;base64," alt="Close view of the A side face with two blow holes marked by the AI" width="680" height="280">
  <div><p class="note" style="margin-bottom:4px">Example from your photos</p><p>The top camera found 2 blow holes on the flat face. The rule allows only 1 there.</p><p class="verdict">Result: FAIL</p></div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="decide">
 <h2>What we still need to decide</h2>
 <ol class="q">
  <li>Where exactly do the four Station 2 cameras sit around the turntable? The drawings show our suggestion: both 0° cameras at the front, the 10° and 45° cameras at the back.</li>
  <li>Should both 0° cameras see every gap (8 stops, as shown), or should each see half of the gaps (4 stops, faster, but each gap is seen from one side only)?</li>
  <li>With the B side up, does CAM 5 need all 8 stops to see the whole bore, or are fewer stops enough? Fewer stops would shorten the cycle.</li>
  <li>Which surfaces may the machine hold or touch without leaving marks?</li>
  <li>One out channel, or separate places for PASS, FAIL and REVIEW parts?</li>
  <li>The camera can measure a hole's size but not its depth. Is size enough for the 1 mm rule?</li>
 </ol>
 <details>
  <summary>Notes for the engineering team</summary>
  <ul>
   <li>Wire each camera's strobe output to its own channel on the light controller. Run the lights in strobe mode, not always on.</li>
   <li>Trigger from the turntable's in-position signal, not from a timer.</li>
   <li>Send each photo during the next 45° turn. Check that the network switch has enough bandwidth for four cameras at once.</li>
   <li>Keep the panel light at Station 1 on its own controller channel too, so it flashes only for the top camera.</li>
   <li>The 45° view may still need two flashes per stop at two brightness levels: darker for the shiny bore, brighter for the rough unwash area.</li>
   <li>Give the AI full-resolution tiles. Shrinking the whole photo to 1280 px makes small pin holes disappear.</li>
   <li>Merge the same defect across both 0° cameras and neighbouring stops before counting, or good parts will fail.</li>
   <li>Teach the AI that marker writing, glare and machining lines are not defects.</li>
   <li>Always save the original photo first. Plan about 0.25 GB of raw photos per part (42 photos; it was about 0.2 GB with 34).</li>
   <li>Keep the station order, stops, camera order, shutter time, flash length and brightness in the recipe file, not in the code.</li>
  </ul>
 </details>
</section>

